# A thin layer over a built TMM image that bakes in the live-surface tools.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE LAYER AND NOT Dockerfile.runtime. The hook index is generated FROM
# the packaged binary, and the packaged binary is produced BY `make container`. Adding
# the index to the runtime Dockerfile would be circular: the file cannot exist until
# the image it belongs in has been built. Layering breaks that cleanly, and it also
# keeps every F5 build file unmodified.
#
# WHY BAKE AT ALL. These used to be kubectl cp'd into running pods. That works and
# reads as a hack: no record, no survival across a restart, different per pod, and it
# happens in front of whoever the demo is for. It also puts files into a
# production-shaped data-plane container by hand, which is its own problem.
#
# WHAT GOES IN, and each is here for a reason rather than convenience:
#
#   hook-index.tsv   name -> entry address, plus the BUILD ID of the binary it was
#                    generated from. ls-load.py refuses to arm when that build id does
#                    not match the running binary. This is the file that turns a
#                    silent mis-arm into an error.
#   signatures.tsv   name -> parameter names, types and kinds, for every function in the
#                    build that takes an argument. Same build id, generated by the same
#                    step. This is what lets mk_probe.py answer "what does this function
#                    take" in 0.10s instead of walking 97 MB of debuginfo for 1m54s ---
#                    which is the difference between a usable tool and a batch job.
#                    IT IS NOT USED BY THE POD. Generating a probe needs clang and
#                    PREVAIL, neither of which belongs in a data-plane container; that
#                    happens on the operator side and the bytecode arrives over the
#                    socket. It is baked in so the index TRAVELS WITH the binary it
#                    describes and can be recovered from the image actually running,
#                    rather than being looked for on whichever build box still has the
#                    DEBs. The build-id gate is what makes that worth doing.
#   ls-load.py       the loader client, versioned. Every live result before it existed
#                    was produced by a client typed inline and thrown away.
#   ls_drain         reads the shared-memory ring and emits JSON records.
#   (no bytecode)    programs are NOT baked --- bytecode is compiled independently and
#                    shipped with the image rather than one carried in.
ARG BASE=tmm:local
FROM ${BASE}

# 0755 on the directory, 0644 on data, 0755 on the two executables. Explicit because
# a COPY inherits the build context's modes, and ls_drain arriving non-executable
# fails at the least convenient moment.
COPY --chmod=0644 hook-index.tsv /usr/share/ls/hook-index.tsv
COPY --chmod=0644 signatures.tsv /usr/share/ls/signatures.tsv
COPY --chmod=0755 ls-load.py     /usr/bin/ls-load.py
COPY --chmod=0755 ls_drain       /usr/bin/ls_drain
# NO bytecode is baked into the image. Compiling bytecode is a COMPLETELY INDEPENDENT process
# (bnk-build-programs.sh: clang -> PREVAIL -> sign, run on its own against any build's BTF); the
# signed .bpf.o is loaded over the socket at runtime and relocated against this binary's embedded
# .BTF. The image carries only build artifacts (binary+VM+.BTF, hook-index, signatures, tools).

# Replace the runtime binary with the one staged by the bake (same build-id; objcopy
# --add-section only appends a section).
#
# WHETHER IT CARRIES `.BTF` IS NOW A CHOICE, and the default is still "yes" --- see
# LS_EMBED_BTF in bnk-bake-tools.sh. With BTF embedded, the loader reads its OWN `.BTF`
# from /proc/self/exe (the kernel's vmlinux model for a userspace process) so the type
# info travels inside the exact binary that gets armed and cannot drift from it. That
# non-drift property is real and is the reason it was done this way.
#
# With LS_EMBED_BTF=0 the section is absent, field offsets are resolved at sign time
# instead, and the loader never reads type information at all
# (02-RESEARCH-PARAMETERS.md P9). That removes 6,711,805 bytes naming 41,710 functions
# and 16,006 struct layouts from the shipped ELF --- the only layout disclosure in the
# image, since F5 ships both binaries `stripped` with symtab FUNC: 0. It also removes
# the non-drift property, which is why the signed build range had to be enforced first
# (CONTESTED-PREMISES.md 15).
COPY --chmod=0755 tmm64.no_pgo /usr/bin/tmm64.no_pgo

# POINT /usr/bin/tmm AT THE PADDED BINARY. Dockerfile.runtime points it at tmm.debug
# whenever a debug binary is present, and the debug build overrides CFLAGS_OPTIMIZE ---
# which is where -fpatchable-function-entry lives. So tmm64.debug has NO entry pads and
# nothing in it can ever be armed; arming fails with "no pad", which reads like a stale
# address and is not. That has now shipped four times, three of them reaching the
# cluster.
#
# Repointing here rather than patching an F5 build file keeps the fix with the thing
# that depends on it: this layer exists to make arming by name work, and arming needs
# the padded binary. tmm64.debug is LEFT IN PLACE --- once tmm does not resolve to it
# its presence is harmless.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-04: this used to call it "a 76 MB debugging aid". It is not a
# debugging aid and carries no debug information --- measured on the pristine F5
# image, it is a second STRIPPED executable from a different build configuration
# (symtab FUNC: 0, no .debug_*, build id 1f7b70d0 vs the running 269b5d25), 76 MB
# against 57 because it is unoptimised. So the reason to keep it is weaker than
# stated and the reason to delete it is different: 76 MB of duplicate, unpadded
# copies of every function is ROP surface. env/scripts/bnk-strip-debug.sh removes
# it; CONTESTED-PREMISES.md 14.
RUN ln -sf /usr/bin/tmm.default /usr/bin/tmm

COPY --chmod=0644 ls_buildid.py      /usr/share/ls/ls_buildid.py
COPY --chmod=0755 ls-verify-layer.sh /usr/share/ls/ls-verify-layer.sh

# Fail HERE, not on the cluster. The build-id comparison is the load-bearing one: it
# proves the index describes the binary this image will actually execute.
#
# As its own script rather than an inline RUN, deliberately --- escaping a multi-line
# Python program through sh inside RUN is how a verification step turns into a
# verification step that always passes.
RUN /usr/share/ls/ls-verify-layer.sh
